Oppose on notability for now. I disagree that the death toll today is the story, or that this is "just another storm" striking a rich country; heavy icing and whiteout conditions are going to disproportionately affect parts of the  [LINK: (Deep South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_South] , Mississippi and Tennessee valleys, and the lower Appalachians, some of the poorest parts of the US. The last I heard, the snow in  [LINK: (Alexander County, Illinois) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_County,_Illinois]  was deep enough that the county issued a travel ban because travel was too dangerous for EMS, and power infrastructure in  [LINK: (Tennessee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee]  was collapsing under the weight of ice, and that wasn't even where the most ice was expected. We'll know for sure in the next week the true extent of this system, but as of now, this is looking most comparable to the  [LINK: (2021 Texas power crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Texas_power_crisis] , but in far more rural and poor regions with steeper terrain that lack the infrastructure to effectively handle such. If this storm only hit, say, the Great Lakes region, we wouldn't be talking about it. Again, we'll know for sure within the next few days once the weather stops; the sheer death toll isn't ever going to be the story, though.  [LINK: (Departure–) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Departure%E2%80%93]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Departure%E2%80%93] )  [LINK: (19:34, 25 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Departure–-20260125193400-Chorchapu-20260125185200] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]

