Comment - original and Alt1/Alt2 are not appropriate If there is a blurb, it should not refer to the storm as having occurred/caused harm "in the United States". Mexico issued weather safty alerts in its northern states and Canadian cities like Toronto have declared the storm a major weather event. We'd also be contradicting the subject article itself, which is called  [LINK: (January 2026 North American winter storm) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_2026_North_American_winter_storm] , and not "January 2026 United States winter storm". There are also reports in Toronto that the storm may have killed one person, with the cause of the vehicle accident being investigated 1. That number could grow. All this to say, the storm has had tough impacts across North America and not just on the United States. I have also gone to the liberty of changing the nomination title accordingly.  [LINK: (Flip) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Flipandflopped]  [LINK: (and) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Contributions/Flipandflopped]  [LINK: (Flopped) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Flipandflopped]   [LINK: (㋡) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Civility]   [LINK: (05:13, 26 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Flipandflopped-20260126051300-Chorchapu-20260125185200] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
I don't see the "sudden death" in "Toronto" being a vehicle accident, in your reference, @ [LINK: (Flipandflopped) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Flipandflopped] . Perhaps it was removed - the "sudden death" wasn't in Toronto but near Highway 7 and 12 - which would be a rural Oshawa area 4 cities further east, and then well north, where there wasn't even that much snow. If only one person in an area were 11 million lived, died in a vehicle accident each day, it would be lower than usual; perhaps because most people stayed off the road - there's no need to blurb that Canadian death. And what has "major weather event" got to do with anything - it's the second one in two weeks; all it means is that there's parking restrictions on major arteries downtown to assist in snow removal and so parked cars don't block streetcars. Yes, Toronto got it's biggest 1-day snowfall downtown ever - just over 2 feet. But there's half to a third of that amount once you get away from the lake - like Highway 7. Does my back hurt yes. Is it ITN - no; there was very little impact to streetcar and bus service even. Maybe if we get a repeat of 1999 with 3 more storms in the next 2 weeks, blocked roads, deaths because people couldn't get to the hospital, and army in the streets (for ER runs) then it's ITN.  [LINK: (Nfitz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Nfitz]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Nfitz] )  [LINK: (06:10, 27 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Nfitz-20260127061000-Flipandflopped-20260126051300] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
