In contrast I have barely heard about it and that's probably due to my geolocation and the  [LINK: (2025–26 European windstorm season) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%9326_European_windstorm_season]  with 471+ fatalities, dwarfing the U.S. snow storm. Per capita, that's roughly twice as many fatalities as the North American storm. Also as an example, BBC coverage of severe weather is dominated by the UK storm. If there is no consensus, it might be due to the varying news on storms being presented geographically.  [LINK: (CNC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:CommunityNotesContributor]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:CommunityNotesContributor] )  [LINK: (14:45, 30 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-CommunityNotesContributor-20260130144500-GenevieveDEon-20260130134000] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
This seems like a poor comparison on its face - the snow storm is an individual one over several days while the season has lasted a few months. However, it should be noted that the majority of fatalities are related to  [LINK: (Storm Harry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%9326_European_windstorm_season#Storm_Harry] , mostly due to potential shipwrecks (with one confirmed killing 50 people and another incident leading to the death of two young children, another with up to 51 deaths, and an estimate of 380 other people missing ref 2) of vessels attempting to cross the Mediterranean from Tunisia. So I think that should have been posted but sadly I don't see very much coverage.  [LINK: (MSG17) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:MSG17]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:MSG17] )  [LINK: (15:44, 30 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-MSG17-20260130154400-CommunityNotesContributor-20260130144500] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]

