I think these sources don't necessarily pose a problem, as the people involved still come to the general conclusion that the Taipei climb should probably be considered more extensive than the Canadian one. The sentence "But Taipei 101 is far from the most challenging urban free solo in history, at least according to Robert’s building scale" gives me more pause, but having an "extremely impressive climb" to feature once every 30 years is pretty fine with me. I am bothered that it seems like specialist magazines don't seem to consider this climb with much enthusiasm, though. I think it's important for us to listen to experts in the field. Climbing does take the climb seriously as an impressive endeavour (see here for an overview article), though it doesn't describe it in any superlative words. Based on my reading of these articles, I still think it's a fine feature, and a spotlight on a much larger field, but it's indeed not quite the perfect feature either. ~ [LINK: (Maplestrip/Mable) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Maplestrip]  ( [LINK: (chat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Maplestrip] )  [LINK: (09:09, 29 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Maplestrip-20260129090900-Nice4What-20260129035100] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]

