Comment In a sense it is indeed in the news a lot, but it feels very American-centric to say this belongs in a list with international conflicts or major protests that have led to massacres. This incident isn't a big deal to anybody but the people in the US. I think the  [LINK: (Greenland crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland_crisis]  has better odds surviving in this category than the United States simply enforcing immigration laws. The only reason I don't say pull entirely is because it has seen highly-publicized shootings such as that of  [LINK: (Renee Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renee_Good]  and  [LINK: (Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_Pretti] , as well as a few other notable happenings such as  [LINK: (Don Lemon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Lemon]  being arrested. Regardless though, I think we should ask ourselves if this is just another classic example of Wikipedia's  [LINK: (Geography bias) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geographical_bias_on_Wikipedia] .

