See  [LINK: (the analysis section) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption_campaign_under_Xi_Jinping#Analysis]  for an extensive discussion of whether it is a purge or some other form of internal conflict.  The word certainly appears in current reporting such as Xi’s Purge of China’s Military Brings Its Top General Down or 'Unprecedented purge': China's No. 1 general under investigation.  [LINK: (Andrew) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Andrew_Davidson] 🐉( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Andrew_Davidson] )  [LINK: (16:08, 24 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Andrew_Davidson-20260124160800-Departure–-20260124153400] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
Looking at that section it is subject to much dispute as to whether this is "purge" or genuine anti-corruption efforts. The framing proposed for the blurb is a total violation of NPOV.  [LINK: (AusLondonder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:AusLondonder]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:AusLondonder] )  [LINK: (16:33, 24 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-AusLondonder-20260124163300-Andrew_Davidson-20260124160800] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
The ordinary meaning of "purge" in this context is "An act of removing objectionable, hostile, or undesirable elements. In later use: esp. the expulsion from an organization, political party, army, etc., of people regarded as undesirable." (OED) Whether that's because of corruption or for other factional reasons, the word works for me either way.  But if you would prefer some other euphemism, please feel free to suggest an alternative.  The major point is that it's a big deal because this is the top general being removed and, according to the article, over two million other officials have been been prosecuted previously. Two million!  How many do we need before we dare to say something?  [LINK: (Andrew) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Andrew_Davidson] 🐉( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Andrew_Davidson] )  [LINK: (17:37, 24 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Andrew_Davidson-20260124173700-AusLondonder-20260124163300] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]

Comment ‘Purge’ seems accurate and is the term being used by RS. The secondary link to an earlier purge from 2012 doesn’t seem quite right, though. This event is part of a newer purge – starting in either 2023 or last autumn (sources differ). At least 14 generals arrested since October’s plenary session, with more reported as ‘missing.’ Zhang’s ‘suspension’ (must we use the CCP’s euphemism?) is notable, but its significance is not yet clear. And may not be clear until RS report on the outcome of 2027’s CCP National Congress or an invasion of Taiwan.  [LINK: (Dr Fell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Dr_Fell&action=edit&redlink=1]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Dr_Fell] )  [LINK: (20:06, 24 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates#c-Dr_Fell-20260124200600-Andrew_Davidson-20260124101900] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:In_the_news/Candidates] ]
